{
  "dom_id": "step-q143109",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis. What differential equation describes this curve?",
  "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *shortest distance from $P(x,y)$ to the $y$-axis * is given by $|x|.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot |x|,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k|x|.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
